1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:09,780
Welcome to the Deep Dive. Today we're, uh, we're looking at how to map out what your ministry or program actually has today, and how to avoid making some incredibly expensive mistakes.

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:15,639
Yeah. We're gonna be unpacking phase one of the P-A-E-R-A or PAERA lifecycle.

3
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:18,040
Right. Specifically, the discover phase.

4
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:25,430
And we'll use our ongoing case study of the Progresa Education Ministry to guide us through how this works in practice.

5
00:00:25,820 --> 00:00:31,710
Okay, let's unpack this because, you know, my natural instinct when looking at an architecture is to focus on what's wrong.

6
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:37,690
Why does this phase focus strictly on what exists today rather than, well, the problems?

7
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:39,660
Yeah. It feels kinda counterintuitive, I know.

8
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:43,080
Hmm. But jumping to conclusions actually biases the entire process.

9
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:48,840
Right. If you start diagnosing issues before you finish mapping the landscape, you miss critical hidden details.

10
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:54,100
In our Progresa case study, this discovery phase takes a tightly boxed three to four weeks.

11
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:55,700
Wow, just three to four weeks.

12
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:59,910
Yeah, it's very focused, and the goal is to produce one single deliverable, the discovery brief.

13
00:01:00,170 --> 00:01:04,200
You're just building an accurate baseline of where the sector is right now, nothing more.

14
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:06,840
So if I'm a director general reviewing this brief,

15
00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:09,180
how do I know it's actually comprehensive?

16
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:13,220
Like, what exactly are we gathering in those few weeks without getting totally bogged down?

17
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:16,880
You keep it focused by collecting five specific elements.

18
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:20,180
Together, they give you the structural DNA of the ministry.

19
00:01:20,630 --> 00:01:26,300
You start with the strategies in force, things like the national digital strategy or the education sector plan.

20
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:27,960
Right, so you understand the official goals.

21
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:28,520
Exactly.

22
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:35,000
Then you map the existing systems. That could be a school census or, you know, a social grant system.

23
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:36,790
And I imagine you also need to know, uh,

24
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:39,120
who actually controls what.

25
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:39,440
Yes.

26
00:01:40,390 --> 00:01:42,600
That leads right into the third and fourth elements.

27
00:01:42,740 --> 00:01:45,170
Right. The registers and their owners. Okay.

28
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:48,550
Like knowing exactly who holds the learner lists or the identity register,

29
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:53,780
and then the broader stakeholders, which includes the ministry authorities and the donors funding each piece.

30
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,160
Got it, and the last one.

31
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,820
Finally, you capture the legal framework, such as data protection acts.

32
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:05,100
You gather these five elements because they define the boundaries of what is legally, technically, and politically possible.

33
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:07,540
Okay. Here is where I wanna push back a little bit.

34
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:12,250
Let's say I'm looking at our architecture, and I see systems that are, um,

35
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:19,460
fragmented, siloed, and require citizen data to be duplicated and rekeyed manually.

36
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:23,220
Right. My instinct is to step in and fix it immediately.

37
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:27,740
Why just gather data when there are glaring, obvious problems?

38
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:31,020
Because the central rule of phase one is to describe

39
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:33,840
without recommending. Just describe. Just describe.

40
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:35,760
Using Progresa as an example,

41
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:43,760
if your architects find three separate learner lists that are, say, locked into one supplier- Yeah... you simply record the fact.

42
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:47,300
You note that they exist, who owns them, and what data they hold.

43
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:50,600
Even if I know it's incredibly costly to change later, I just write it down?

44
00:02:50,660 --> 00:02:57,080
You just record the facts. The judgment of whether this is a problem or what priority it takes belongs entirely to the next phase.

45
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:58,799
Discover first, judge second.

46
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:00,600
That requires some serious discipline.

47
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:05,740
It really does. Yeah. If you mix the two, you end up designing solutions before you fully map the environment.

48
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:09,350
Okay, so what does this all mean for actually finishing the phase?

49
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:14,800
How do we know when this discovery brief is done, and we can finally move on to judging the systems?

50
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:18,080
It ends with the first formal sign-off.

51
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:27,690
A senior decision maker, such as the digitalization officer who chairs the National Enterprise Architecture, or EA board, signs off on one single metric.

52
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:29,790
Which is? That the picture is accurate.

53
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:32,200
Just accurate. It doesn't need to be, like, complete.

54
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:35,980
I would think a director general wants a perfect map before making any decisions.

55
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:38,440
Well, this raises an important question about scale.

56
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:41,570
No, it does not need to be perfectly complete.

57
00:03:41,940 --> 00:03:44,240
It just needs to be accurate enough to build on.

58
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:47,680
Uh. This approval gate is cheap and fast to pass through.

59
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,910
Skipping it, however, is incredibly expensive.

60
00:03:50,020 --> 00:03:52,180
Because if your foundation is off...

61
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:57,190
Exactly. If you build an assessment on a wrong picture, you produce wrong priorities.

62
00:03:57,329 --> 00:04:03,820
Mm-hmm. And redirecting an entire ministry after it has started acting on wrong priorities is profoundly costly to change.

63
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:08,230
Yeah, that makes total sense. Get the baseline signed off as true, and the next phase stands on solid ground.

64
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:15,510
Precisely. That clarifies a lot. So to bring this all together for our Progresa case study, how would you summarize the core rules of this first phase?

65
00:04:15,860 --> 00:04:19,950
Phase one comes down to four pillars. One question, what exists today?

66
00:04:20,820 --> 00:04:21,490
One deliverable,

67
00:04:22,350 --> 00:04:24,550
the discovery brief. Right. One discipline,

68
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:27,650
describe, do not recommend. And one sign-off,

69
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:29,409
the picture is accurate.

70
00:04:29,430 --> 00:04:30,800
You gather, you do not judge.

